Z||000000||MikeE||05-25-2000||07:52 AM||||Hello everyone<P>I both found and joined this forum today although I have been a Fiero owner for several years.  I found the link to this site on the Quad4 forum when Paul Herder posted a link to pictures of his Quad4 Fiero.<P>I live in Hawaii and for some reason, Fieros are a dime a dozen here, perhaps becase there are so many of them.  Unfortunately, that situation may not continue for long because this is a small island with limited space and consequently many cars, including Fieros, ultimately meet the crusher.<P>I bought my first Fiero, an 86 2m4 SC, purposely with a bad engine, intending to swap in a Quad4.  Shortly thereafter I found a wrecked 89 Quad 4 Grand Am with only 30K Mi. for a good price.  However, before I could finalize the deal, the seller lost the car to a towing co. who promptly tripled the price.<P>While searching for another donor I came across two Fieros in a tow yard that had come from one of the many military bases here.  I paid only $500 for both of them plus $80 to tow them to my place.  Both were 87 models, one a 2m4/5sp, the other a 2m6/5sp SE.  Using a jumper wire, I quickly discovered that the clutch disconnect switch on the V6 was shorted out so the 86 became a parts car and donated its switch.  The car ran great right up until I sold it in Sept. 99.  Unfortunately, the first day I put it on the street after it was licensed and insured, someone backed into it while I was at school.  A neighbor saw it but could only tell me it was a white taxi and there are thousands of those where I lived in Waikiki.  It only cracked a fender and dinged the front valence though.  I'd fixed enough surfboards to make easy work of it.<P>I sold it because we moved and lost one parking space.  I figured the running V6 would sell a lot quicker than the non-running 4 that also needs a clutch.  In truth, since my daughter was born I seldom got to drive it since I can't take the family in a two seater and the Fiero seat belts won't secure a child seat anyway.<P>I still have the 87 2m4 and I was thinking of doing an engine swap but I'm moving to the mainland for grad school at the end of the year.  The clutch is no problem but I'd like to get it running right so any advice would be helpful.  I will start but it takes a long time and then it hunts up and down while idling.  It dies immediately when any throttle is applied.  I ran the codes and it indicated to replace the O2 sensor but I don't think that's all there is to it.  I don't no how long it sat in the tow yard so I'm going to purge the fuel tank and give it fresh gas.  Does anyone have any thoughts?  Thanks and sorry this is so long, I'll keep them shorter in the future.<P>Mike||||reg||6
Z||000001||ChadMan||05-25-2000||09:48 AM||||Welcome, Mike!  I can only imagine how cool it would be to drive around Oahu in a Fiero...<P>But back to your problem.  I assume your 87 2M4 is distributorless, and I also assume you're confident both coils and plug wires are good.  The 02 sensor probably needs replaced, but I doubt it could cause the problems you're describing.  I'd check out your MAP sensor, TPS, and IAC valve.  Purging the tank is a good idea - if your car sat long enough for the gas to start turning to varnish, your fuel filter and/or injector could be clogged, too.  <P>Long shot - there's a crankshaft position sensor that mounts under the coilpak/ignition module assembly - I think the DIS motors use information from it to set and advance their own timing.  If it were bad, the engine couldn't advance its timing upon sudden throttle inputs.  But the CPS is a pretty simple device and it almost never fails.<P>Have you gotten any other trouble codes besides the O2 sensor code?||||reg||1
